{"records":[{"id":"jxrx3m6ysk","title":"Multi-omics data (phospholipidomic, metabolomic and transcriptomic data) of postnatal microglia","description":"Microglia undergo morphological and functional changes during development, yet little is known about the metabolic programs that shape microglial development. Here, using multi-omics profiling, we generate a landscape of membrane phospholipids and cellular metabolites across microglial development. We reveal sequential shifts in microglial metabolome and phospholipidome during development and uncover critical metabolic features of microglia at early postnatal stages. Distinct from adult microglia utilizing glutamine in the absence of glucose, early postnatal microglia exhibit high uptake and demands for glutamine. Blocking glutaminolysis significantly suppresses early postnatal microglial proliferation and phagocytosis  in vivo and in vitro. Isotope tracing demonstrates that glutamine is preferentially fluxed to aspartate, and inhibiting aspartate conversion attenuates microglial proliferation but not phagocytosis, suggesting glutamine fuels into divergent metabolic pathways to support distinct microglial functions. Our study reveals developmentally-regulated metabolic features of microglia and highlights glutamine metabolism as a key player in shaping early microglia.\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-12T11:10:18.67Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jxrx3m6ysk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jxrx3m6ysk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fang","last_name":"Wang","orcid":"0000-0001-8035-2855"},{"first_name":"Yuxiang","last_name":"Luo","orcid":"0000-0003-1190-5887"},{"first_name":"Zhihua","last_name":"Gao","orcid":"0000-0001-8603-0777"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microglia","Lipidomics","Metabolomics","RNA Sequencing","Phospholipid Metabolism","Metabolism","Brain Development"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4gg9mzth9x","title":"Ageing_data","description":"Data from: Early-Life Prebiotics Shape Microbial Metabolism and Microglial Redox State to Preserve Social Motivation During Ageing","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-05T13:32:08.657Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4gg9mzth9x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4gg9mzth9x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Eric B","last_name":"Daliri","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/02gs16m83","name":"Lietuvos Mokslo Taryba","grant_id":"S-PD-24-153"}],"categories":["Data Access"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gksmyhy89r","title":"miR-181a Restores Mitochondrial Dynamics in Skeletal Muscle Ageing","description":"Label free proteomic data associated with this study, the raw data has been deposited to the ProteomeXchange Consortium via the PRIDE partner repository with the dataset identifier PXD010091.\nIncluded in the Appenndix are the sequences of primers used for qPCR and sequences of murine p62 or Park2 3’UTRs or DJ-1 Exon 6 (wild type – WT or with mutations introduced into themiR-181a binding site – mutant) cloned into the GFP reporter constructs.\nIn appendix the original western blots related to this data with a merged image of chemiluminescence and bright field indicating protein ladder.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-10-22T08:14:25.399Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gksmyhy89r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gksmyhy89r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Katarzyna","last_name":"Goljanek-Whysall","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ana","last_name":"Soriano--Arroquia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rachel","last_name":"McCormick","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caroline","last_name":"Chinda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brian","last_name":"McDonagh","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Polymerase Chain Reaction","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dvmp9g5dt9","title":"Targeting APT2 to Enhance Tau Palmitoylation and Reprogram the Neuroimmune Landscape for Cognitive Restoration in Alzheimer's Disease","description":"Alzheimer's disease (AD) remains a formidable therapeutic challenge, largely because interventions targeting isolated pathological features have shown limited efficacy. Here, we identify dynamic palmitoylation of Tau as a regulatory mechanism that integrates the core pathological triad of AD: Tau dysfunction, neuroinflammation, and synaptic impairment. Tau palmitoylation is governed by the palmitoyltransferase ZDHHC23 and the depalmitoylating enzyme APT2. In both 5xFAD mice and human AD brains, Tau palmitoylation is markedly diminished. Mechanistically, without altering Aβ levels, genetic or pharmacological inhibition of APT2 enhances Tau palmitoylation, stabilizes Tau–microtubule interactions, suppresses disulfide bond–mediated oligomerization, and restores neuronal morphology and cognitive function. Single-nucleus RNA sequencing reveals that APT2 loss reprograms neuronal and microglial populations, underscoring the convergence of inflammatory and synaptic pathways. Notably, enhancing Tau palmitoylation in neurons remodels the neuroimmune landscape and rescues cognition independently of changes in amyloid-beta levels. These findings position APT2 inhibition as a promising therapeutic strategy for AD.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-23T20:10:58.015Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dvmp9g5dt9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dvmp9g5dt9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"攀","last_name":"王","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Neuron"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"czh96my8cf","title":"Enhancing microglial phagocytosis analysis through deep learning: A U-Net based approach","description":"This dataset provides a trained U-Net (called AIstain), an DEMO analysis assay and the training dataset used in our corresponding Manuscript: \"Enhancing microglial phagocytosis analysis through deep learning: A U-Net based approach\" (Zähringer et al.) submitted to Cell Reports Methods.\nThe neural network can be used on Olympus ScanR systems with the analysis software v3.4 and higher to perform label-free live cell imaging experiments.\nFurther information and how to use the U-Net can be found in the publication.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-22T13:09:04.896Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/czh96my8cf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/czh96my8cf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"Zähringer","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bioinformatics","Deep Learning","Biomedical Research","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"whdgg3tfmt","title":"Infection-induced vascular inflammation in COVID-19 links focal microglial dysfunction with neuropathologies through IL-1/IL-6-related systemic inflammatory states","description":"COVID-19 is associated with diverse neurological abnormalities, which predict poor outcome in patients. However, the mechanisms whereby infection-induced inflammation could affect complex neuropathologies in COVID-19 are unclear. We hypothesized that microglia, the resident immune cells of brain, are centrally involved in this process. To study this, we developed an autopsy platform allowing the integration of molecular anatomy-, protein- and mRNA data sets in post-mortem mirror blocks of brain and peripheral organ samples from COVID-19 cases. Nanoscale microscopy, single-cell RNA sequencing and analysis of inflammatory and metabolic signatures revealed distinct mechanisms of microglial dysfunction associated with cerebral SARS-CoV-2 infection. We observed focal loss of microglial P2Y12R at sites of virus-associated vascular inflammation together with dysregulated microglia-vascular-astrocyte interactions, CX3CR1-CX3CL1 axis deficits and metabolic failure in severely affected medullary autonomic nuclei and other brain areas. Microglial dysfunction associated with mitochondrial injury and cell loss occurs at sites of excessive synapse- and myelin phagocytosis and loss of glutamatergic terminals in line with proteomic changes of synapse assembly, metabolism and neuronal injury. These changes parallel increased numbers of perivascular macrophages in the medulla. While central and systemic viral load is strongly linked in individual patients, the regionally heterogenous microglial reactivity in the brain correlated with the extent of central and systemic inflammation related to IL-1 / IL-6 via virus-sensing pattern recognition receptors (PRRs) and inflammasome activation pathways. Thus, SARS-CoV-2-induced central and systemic inflammation might lead to a primarily glio-vascular failure in the brain, which could be a common contributor to diverse COVID-19-related neuropathologies.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-11-18T14:16:47.536Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/whdgg3tfmt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/whdgg3tfmt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rebeka","last_name":"Fekete","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alba","last_name":"Simats","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Arthur","last_name":"Liesz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ádám","last_name":"Dénes","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Inflammation","Microglia","Neuro-Inflammation","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gnfnxs565b","title":"Electroacupuncture Preconditioning Attenuates Myocardial Ischemia-Reperfusion Injury by Inhibiting Microglial Engulfment of Dendritic Spines in the Fastigial Nucleus","description":"A major side effect of reperfusion therapy following myocardial infarction is myocardial ischemia-reperfusion injury (MIRI). Electroacupuncture preconditioning (EA-pre) has a long history in the treatment of cardiovascular diseases. Here, we demonstrate how EA-pre attenuates MIRI by affecting the phagocytosis of neuronal dendritic spines of microglia of the fastigial nucleus (FNmicroglia). From optical-fiber-based Ca2+ signal recordings in GABAergic neurons of the FN (FNGABA), we observed that EA-pre for 7 days increased activity in FNGABA and then improved myocardial injury by inhibiting abnormal activities of glutaminergic neurons of the FN (FNGlu ) during MIRI. Interestingly, we observed changes in the quantity and shape of FN microglia in mice treated with EA-pre, and a decrease in the phagocytosis of FNGABA neuronal dendritic spines by microglia. Furthermore, the effects of improving MIRI were reversed when EA-pre mice were chemically activated by intra-FN lipopolysaccharide injection, followed by a reduction in FNGABA activity. Overall, our results provide new insight indicating that EA-pre negatively regulates microglial engulfment capacity, thus promoting the improvement of cardiac sympathetic nervous injury and disorders during MIRI.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-04-13T16:28:10.062Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gnfnxs565b","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gnfnxs565b"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"fan","last_name":"ZHANG","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Video","Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9kdc2gb2pw","title":"Electroacupuncture Preconditioning Attenuates Myocardial Ischemia-Reperfusion Injury by Inhibiting Microglial Engulfment of Dendritic Spines in the Fastigial Nucleus","description":"A major side effect of reperfusion therapy following myocardial infarction is myocardial ischemia-reperfusion injury (MIRI). Electroacupuncture preconditioning (EA-pre) has a long history in the treatment of cardiovascular diseases. Here, we demonstrate how EA-pre attenuates MIRI by affecting the phagocytosis of neuronal dendritic spines of microglia of the fastigial nucleus (FNmicroglia). From optical-fiber-based Ca2+ signal recordings in GABAergic neurons of the FN (FNGABA), we observed that EA-pre for 7 days increased activity in FNGABA and then improved myocardial injury by inhibiting abnormal activities of glutaminergic neurons of the FN (FNGlu) during MIRI. Interestingly, we observed changes in the quantity and shape of FN microglia in mice treated with EA-pre, and a decrease in the phagocytosis of FNGABA neuronal dendritic spines by microglia. Furthermore, the effects of improving MIRI were reversed when EA-pre mice were chemically activated by intra-FN lipopolysaccharide injection, followed by a reduction in FNGABA activity. Overall, our results provide new insight indicating that EA-pre negatively regulates microglial engulfment capacity, thus promoting the improvement of cardiac sympathetic nervous injury and disorders during MIRI.\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-28T10:28:03.261Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9kdc2gb2pw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9kdc2gb2pw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"fan","last_name":"ZHANG","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j6b64zhmx8","title":"Electroacupuncture Preconditioning Attenuates Myocardial Ischemia-Reperfusion Injury by Inhibiting Microglial  Engulfment of Dendritic Spines in the Fastigial Nucleus  ","description":"A major side effect of reperfusion therapy following myocardial infarction is myocardial ischemia-reperfusion injury (MIRI). Electroacupuncture preconditioning (EA-pre) has a long history in the treatment of cardiovascular diseases. Here, we demonstrate how EA-pre attenuates MIRI by affecting the phagocytosis of neuronal dendritic spines of microglia of the fastigial nucleus (FNmicroglia). From optical-fiber-based Ca2+ signal recordings in GABAergic neurons of the FN (FNGABA), we observed that EA-pre for 7 days increased activity in FNGABA and then improved myocardial injury by inhibiting abnormal activities of glutaminergic neurons of the FN (FNGlu) during MIRI. Interestingly, we observed changes in the quantity and shape of FN microglia in mice treated with EA-pre, and a decrease in the phagocytosis of FNGABA neuronal dendritic spines by microglia. Furthermore, the effects of improving MIRI were reversed when EA-pre mice were chemically activated by intra-FN lipopolysaccharide injection, followed by a reduction in FNGABA activity. Overall, our results provide new insight indicating that EA-pre negatively regulates microglial engulfment capacity, thus promoting the improvement of cardiac sympathetic nervous injury and disorders during MIRI.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-28T16:25:14.664Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j6b64zhmx8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j6b64zhmx8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"fan","last_name":"ZHANG","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zv7m7wb2cy","title":"Electroacupuncture Preconditioning Attenuates Myocardial Ischemia-Reperfusion Injury by Inhibiting Microglial Engulfment of Dendritic Spines in the Fastigial Nucleus ","description":"A major side effect of reperfusion therapy following myocardial infarction is myocardial ischemia-reperfusion injury (MIRI). Electroacupuncture preconditioning (EA-pre) has a long history in the treatment of cardiovascular diseases. Here, we demonstrate how EA-pre attenuates MIRI by affecting the phagocytosis of neuronal dendritic spines of microglia of the fastigial nucleus (FNmicroglia). From optical-fiber-based Ca2+ signal recordings in GABAergic neurons of the FN (FNGABA), we observed that EA-pre for 7 days increased activity in FNGABA and then improved myocardial injury by inhibiting abnormal activities of glutaminergic neurons of the FN (FNGlu) during MIRI. Interestingly, we observed changes in the quantity and shape of FN microglia in mice treated with EA-pre, and a decrease in the phagocytosis of FNGABA neuronal dendritic spines by microglia. Furthermore, the effects of improving MIRI were reversed when EA-pre mice were chemically activated by intra-FN lipopolysaccharide injection, followed by a reduction in FNGABA activity. Overall, our results provide new insight indicating that EA-pre negatively regulates microglial engulfment capacity, thus promoting the improvement of cardiac sympathetic nervous injury and disorders during MIRI.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-28T10:31:59.637Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zv7m7wb2cy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zv7m7wb2cy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"fan","last_name":"ZHANG","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Database"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":104787,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":91,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":104787,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":104351,"restricted":53,"embargoed":383},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1839},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":542},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":421},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":357},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":357},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":352},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":325},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":287},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":222},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":189}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":102283},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1883},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":166},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":107},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":64},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":61},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":45},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":29},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":27},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":443},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":377},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":326},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":263},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":253}]}}